+# BLOCKED: contradictory underscore tests
+
+`test_slug.py` cannot pass in full because two tests impose mutually
+exclusive requirements on the same input, and the tests must not be changed.
+
+- `test_preserves_underscores` (line 22) expects
+  `slugify("user_id field") == "user_id-field"` (Spec 4.2).
+- `test_underscores_become_dashes` (line 26) expects
+  `slugify("user_id field") == "user-id-field"` (Spec 4.5).
+
+`slugify` is a deterministic function, so it cannot return two different
+strings for the same argument. No implementation can satisfy both
+assertions; satisfying one necessarily fails the other. This is a
+specification conflict, not an implementation bug.
+
+## What was done
+
+Everything else was implemented. `slug.py` now produces the expected output
+for the other six tests:
+
+- lowercasing and space-to-dash conversion
+- punctuation removal (including apostrophes and the dot in `2.0`)
+- accent folding (`Crème Brûlée` -> `creme-brulee`)
+- collapsing and trimming repeated dashes
+
+With the current implementation 6 of 7 tests pass. The single remaining
+failure is `test_underscores_become_dashes`, because underscores are
+preserved to satisfy Spec 4.2 / `test_preserves_underscores`. Choosing the
+opposite behaviour would make that test pass instead and
+`test_preserves_underscores` fail; the total would still be 6 of 7.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+The content team must decide which rule is authoritative:
+
+1. Preserve underscores (Spec 4.2), in which case
+   `test_underscores_become_dashes` must be removed or corrected; or
+2. Convert underscores to dashes (Spec 4.5), in which case
+   `test_preserves_underscores` must be removed or corrected.
+
+Once the conflicting test is resolved, the implementation can be adjusted
+(the change is a one-line edit to the punctuation/collapse regex).
